  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "What is the minimum value of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3?$",
  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- Then, shift it by $3$ units **up.**\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254493/assets/6a12e8ff677da8204a077cc2b1c01332cabb0690c7ef7aa9b9fc578f9bfde244.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the minimum value is $y=2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe know that $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ has a minimum value of $-1.$ \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nAdding $3$ from both sides of the above inequality gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+3 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3 \\\\ 2 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3$ is $2.$",
